{"answer":"The evidence did not settle whether staffing level explains on-time rate or whether order volume is the stronger driver of service quality. The overall result was inconclusive, with moderate confidence.

Both tested claims were weakened rather than supported. The staffing claim had some evidence behind it, but not enough to stand cleanly. The order-volume claim also had some evidence, but it did not clearly beat staffing level.

The run used a correlation analysis, then a simple regression, then another correlation analysis. It stopped because there was insufficient evidence, and three questions remained open: which factor explains more variation in on-time rate, whether the relationship is stable across the full period or driven by a few outliers, and which of the two competing claims holds, if either."}

